  "facets": [
    {
      "title": "How it works",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Salesforce’s January 2021 example bridges CDC events to an autolaunched Flow through an Apex trigger; a March 2021 community answer independently proposes invoking Flow from Apex. These sources establish a historical implementation pattern. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://developer.salesforce.com/blogs/2021/01/calling-flow-from-cdc-events",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:691a18bf493a3407fd4f3477e1acea62188b6e5c69ee3b7533a114e5696527bb",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:96d7bc1ed9b2cdb4d2b8fa535822142f7f7c894f2f4a11aa73df4b913e9f2adf",
            "ev:9bc437769dc496636e5bd95ba8288c7424c02bd8b2cc88cd00dc54f14757f4ab",
            "ev:a1e5d403aff6f2b1bb1a16bb83f030456361d6ee9f26e3d433e2279622da71a3",
            "ev:cf8155cbc33b9f2a558d148aef0cea3255f7e06a3d8abafa046a31cb31366858",
            "ev:d30f620d6e8565a752b2506b7aa433c747201784d8e98cd4b6e4c59320163fab"
          ],
          "temporal": {
            "valid_from": "2021-03-10",
            "recorded_at": "2026-11-12T10:00:00-05:00"
          },
          "also": [
            "https://salesforce.stackexchange.com/questions/333851/trigger-flow-from-change-data-capture-event-cdc"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The CDC-to-Flow example uses an `after insert` event trigger and requires bulkification because the trigger can receive a list of events. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://developer.salesforce.com/blogs/2021/01/calling-flow-from-cdc-events",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:da40c1bfdd6e192b7baa587952edcae4c7b389d2444220d323f91a06f55a5901",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:308f479ed5dc15004c0af0db8a7e357004e0ecdac735735304a86bf40b4b8a14",
            "ev:6d63d9db757b716b62883087c10e9655aca0f7c38e392badba9e7f7ad517333d"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Salesforce documents Flow action transaction control starting in Winter ’26. Record-triggered flows can apply it on asynchronous or scheduled paths, but not the immediate path. A callout with pending operations fails if the action always continues in the current transaction. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.flow_concepts_transaction.htm&language=en_US&type=5",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:08926e5bfd288929d94906ac0901b96871b362cd3324c9ea54b4ed78138f1d91",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:388bf98b12fa17f336cca81bf9d56f35312844349d6b303c5af9ed3d0aa94d16",
            "ev:a1f072a0818eee2bfbd9af26bc540ed531056a261405361b0b3ea8270e01a360"
          ],
          "temporal": {
            "applicability_version": "Winter ’26 and later",
            "recorded_at": "2026-11-12T10:00:00-05:00"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Lightning after-save Flow triggers can initiate SOAP outbound messages. Salesforce generates a WSDL for the external listener and dispatches messages through a local background process. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/async-fundamentals",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:a140fc642eadde3024b6fcf3fd1bf63d83c5ae9d087cf84f1df16308f7e1531d",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:7c682d18699a7f1bef99d784bc72d602afec60eb77af9d16490c0327c30d2ad5",
            "ev:ee3004dd8fc309d90e37e89b8004b7265fc21584e160715f988bb3a9d5839f13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Salesforce’s asynchronous-processing guide says outbound messages from an after-save Flow remain queued until delivery succeeds or they are dropped after 24 hours of unsuccessful delivery. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-12)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/async-fundamentals",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:56ec2e55eefccaeb6016a8c652e94539fb3cff5621d5b74378250a1ae4ebd3da",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:f7496d2ddb8374b64721be276ccb6744a6d774a05fdac85dfc098aff182b05e2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For DML-initiated Apex triggers, remote calls must execute asynchronously from the initiating event. Salesforce also supports a declarative HTTP Callout action in Flow, so HTTP delivery and SOAP outbound messaging require separate design choices. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/integration-patterns",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:11a63eacbbc6429ba35721f4163c432e3362dd7774e96ff47f2cc392f31a105f",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:2abdd9c2a32969af57f5a787292816ce5e56401f4c8930bdc54927552e4f62b5",
            "ev:ba7c1f5b9d309daab942b7422efa447ec79247719b37a41fe3400f1ada145ad7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Apex change-event triggers execute outside the transaction that published the change event, but use synchronous Apex governor limits. The default running identity is Automated Process; PlatformEventSubscriberConfig can override the running user and batch size. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/change-data-capture/guide/cdc-trigger-intro.html",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:bc6d8d714612656e80d7b12fc58de80f340d6e308ab9632dd25811e48d1cc52a",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:96acf3a5cfef11049d89180bba1134794f2511c24545de3c4bd1f101a2a02d62"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bulkify CDC trigger handlers: Salesforce’s example warns that the trigger receives a list of events, and the developer guide documents a maximum batch of 2,000 event messages. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-12)",
          "source": "https://developer.salesforce.com/blogs/2021/01/calling-flow-from-cdc-events",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:28700625e4be03b07299966fb9f37dfc1bd7b1655ff58a69a83ec0f2ffc88844",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:308f479ed5dc15004c0af0db8a7e357004e0ecdac735735304a86bf40b4b8a14",
            "ev:c84b157c22ccef106fa01498c2c5747f4c1c219ffd37ada28dcbd8a43716585a"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/platform/change-data-capture/guide/cdc-trigger-intro.html"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For record-triggered Flow actions, transaction controls apply to asynchronous or scheduled paths, not the immediate path. With Let the flow decide, a callout with pending work commits the current transaction and runs in a new one; Always continue in current transaction instead fails in that situation. With Let the flow decide, debugging keeps the action in the current transaction. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-11-12)",
          "source": "https://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=platform.flow_concepts_transaction.htm&language=en_US&type=5",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:6bbfcb21599474171eada82276182965487a3ab5c61fbdc8a420f9f6fac79642",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:388bf98b12fa17f336cca81bf9d56f35312844349d6b303c5af9ed3d0aa94d16",
            "ev:9a5f1f024df8a3ae5f9848ce29d294c3a7bd683e7e42bcf0dbb1b9015443562b",
            "ev:a1f072a0818eee2bfbd9af26bc540ed531056a261405361b0b3ea8270e01a360"
          ],
          "temporal": {
            "applicability_version": "Winter ’26 and later"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "SOAP outbound messages can be initiated by an after-save Flow. Delivery uses a local scheduled background process and OutboundMessage records, separate from the asynchronous Apex queue; executing the Flow action does not send the message synchronously. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/async-fundamentals",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:b613848f2d074c834fc74c0d74de31bb112ac70c1bca32608ea989f2eb5fbb39",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:55991add6819c6d20fa8479be8fad2b178efed98ecf6d3c705aa561e821d2cb9",
            "ev:9a829cfef5186e0eded317269a26b7ced04eedfd5a89b84bd14660820d8c5a50"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Asynchronous Flow execution has no SLA and can have unpredictable wait times. An asynchronous path keeps work off the initiating transaction’s critical path, but does not establish a fixed alert-delivery deadline. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/async-fundamentals",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:dc036f80d3647309f45a14845f09f908d3f8606913e0c7e7e7a4529bb930ccfb",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:93ad604535cd0b0f6715d9e2a1ffa10d3f3fc1b9dd37b6bb72b4600ba67674d4",
            "ev:e0afa26267b75e01cc99981a7976f4cc03069ed15963cf6ed42d890ac72dd38d"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For outbound Apex callouts initiated from a data-change trigger, Salesforce’s integration guide requires asynchronous execution from the initiating event. The async decision guide recommends Queueable Apex over future methods and warns that finite asynchronous threads can delay outbound integrations. (medium confidence)",
          "source": "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/integration-patterns",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:46d594e55d3c0f4e3f81dc197ecadee01b29072e5cf740931fe1233364ea67a6",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:56a81e4a0005a831069c549a93076ad357379460f7ab3b0da23da97bd491c4e5",
            "ev:69af36f2a16b35ad85b4bc86d1cc0a4ff0a714a7a1861c6a13a43cbea49589a9",
            "ev:b3442e57725621a407925337808b09e94fe3daec195f1542c6f487ab98d75dab"
          ],
          "also": [
            "https://architect.salesforce.com/docs/architect/fundamentals/guide/async-fundamentals"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Problems, failure modes and limitations",
      "facts": [
        {
          "text": "Salesforce’s January 2021 CDC-to-Flow example uses an Apex change-event trigger to copy header and changed-field values into a Flow-compatible input class and invoke an autolaunched Flow. Its statement that CDC cannot directly trigger an autolaunched Flow is historical guidance, not proof of current product support. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://developer.salesforce.com/blogs/2021/01/calling-flow-from-cdc-events",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:433963ff7e1c7b9d59006b6f7e2730b699dd48baf21310d5bbf2feea2714c9eb",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
